book I've read in decades. The man is so smart it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:46.320
will scare you but one of the major theses of&nbsp;
the book is that when we solve problems there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:53.840
are two ways we can go. We can think about it&nbsp;
very very deeply or we can use our intuition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:01.600
A good part of the research that he's done says if&nbsp;
you ask people how they process they say deeply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.840
if you watch the solutions they come up&nbsp;
with they're almost always intuitive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:17.520
Intuition sounds like a flighty kind of idea&nbsp;
but intuition is based on everything you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:26.000
everything you believe, all of your history, so&nbsp;
it's a vast storehouse of information. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:35.840
not always starting from scratch the interesting&nbsp;
thing about this is if you're pitching anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:44.400
screenplay a new detergent doesn't matter you&nbsp;
start out with the idea that you'd like to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:51.760
people processing the information you're telling&nbsp;
them at the intuitive level because if it's in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:59.440
intuitive level then everything is fine everything&nbsp;
goes and fits with your background and everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:06.480
you believe the minute you make somebody go&nbsp;
into deep hard thought one you don't like it&nbsp;&nbsp;

